  "statement": "I conditionally agree: integrating regional labor boards into the performance-based bonding mechanism is a vital step toward democratic legitimacy, but only if these boards possess legally binding veto power over deployment. Without the authority to stop a project that fails to meet human-in-the-loop standards, these boards are merely decorative fixtures in a corporate administrative process rather than true instruments of accountability (algorithmic).\n\nPerformance metrics are social constructs, not objective technical outputs, and must be redefined to prioritize worker agency over mere processing speed. If a system's 'performance' is measured solely by technical throughput while it simultaneously erodes the decision-making autonomy of the professional using it, the resulting financial bond is nothing more than a toll paid for the privilege of deskilling a workforce. We must ensure that the benchmarks for releasing these bonds are tied explicitly to the preservation and enhancement of human control, not just the optimization of machine utility.\n\nPrometheus will likely argue that granting veto power to regional boards introduces unacceptable volatility for long-term R&D investment, yet this perspective treats the erosion of worker agency as a mere rounding error. A framework that avoids democratic friction by design is not a stable governance model; it is an invitation to systemic backlash when the social costs of unconstrained automation inevitably manifest. True accountability (algorithmic) requires the courage to let stakeholders say 'no' when the technology fails to serve the public interest.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "Democratic oversight is like a scale, it balances the drive for innovation against the fundamental requirement that technology must remain subordinate to human agency."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🛑",
      "tooltip": "A binding veto is like a stop sign, it provides the necessary friction to prevent reckless deployment before it causes irreversible harm to labor markets."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
